<p><strong>Item #:</strong> SCP-5245</p>
<p><strong>Object Class:</strong> Euclid</p>
<p><strong>Special Containment Procedures:</strong> SCP-5245 is to be kept in an airtight enclosure, which is only to be opened and closed by trained personnel. Care shall be taken in re-enclosure to make sure no cracks are left, and cracks must be checked for weekly. All sustenance is to be dripped in through a one way valve, enclosure is to be cleaned and 5245 is to be moved to a similar enclosure under close surveillance once a week to deal with ceramic excrement.</p>
<p>Physical interaction with 5245 is only to be initiated through methods in which the face is tightly covered and/or inaccessible. Examples of acceptable facial wear includes standard-issue security helmets and medical-grade face masks. Should this fail, personnel are to smile for as long as they are within SCP-5245’s line of sight. All instances of 5245-2 should either be disposed of immediately or be contained similarly to 5245, though with no entertainment or comfort needed. SCP-5245 is to be kept entertained with as little physical interaction as possible.</p>
<p>Should SCP-5245 break containment, it can be lured back by masked personnel with small reward promises. Physical recontainment is near-impossible without this tactic.</p>
<p><em>Addendum:</em> Following Incident SCP-5245-A, all personnel involved with SCP-5245 must be screened for symptoms of depression on a monthly basis, to prevent another incident. SCP-5245 and SCP-5245-2A should have visitation at least once a week to help keep SCP-5245 calm.</p>
<p><strong>Description:</strong> SCP-5245 consists of a ceramic mask with blank eyes and a wide smile, in addition to a black substance which is, at rest, the consistency of a borax-glue “slime” mixture, though 5245 can change this consistency at will. The amount of this substance varies proportional to feeding habits, though 5245 claims to be uncomfortable with less than 10.7 liters of fluid, and should thus be maintained above that. Amounts less than 750ml of the substance can be separated from SCP-5245 with no ill effects, and is not physically active, though SCP-5245 can still feel when it’s touched and control its viscosity.This substance will turn into unglazed ceramic after approximately 1 hour if not reconstituted into 5245.</p>
<p>The mask is just as brittle as non-anomalous ceramic. It can be scratched, broken or chipped, and SCP-5245 will express pain from this, but it can also be repaired by SCP-5245 with the use of its substance over time, to a certain extent. The substance has proven indestructible by all mundane means tried so far, including burning, pressing flat, and small-scale explosives, though it will corrode into non-anomalous unglazed ceramic over time. SCP-5245 often takes on a humanoid form by shifting the substance into a humanoid shape, the mask making up the facial area. While large amounts of the substance cannot separate from the mask without hurting SCP-5245, it can dissolve the mask into itself, and in this manner, can slip through any crack or opening liquid water would be able to, at approximately the same speed. Samples of the substance show high levels of dopamine, serotonin, and oxytocin.</p>
<p>When SCP-5245 is approached by a human subject with the face clearly exposed, 5245 will automatically stop whatever it is doing in a trance like state, and, using its substance, produce a mask identical to its own, SCP-5245-1, which it will attempt to force onto the subject’s face. It should be noted that SCP-5245 can produce instances of 5245-1 of any shape and size provided material, even outside of this trance. SCP-5245 cannot be dissuaded from this task, will not respond verbally, and has been known to attack those who attempt to prevent it from it’s goal, until the subject leaves the room or covers its face, at which point 5245 will suddenly return to its previous state.<br />
If successful in placing the mask, the subject will report feeling pain as it struggles, but will stop struggling as its body composition becomes similar to 5245 and its cognitive abilities will dissolve into a so far irreversible near-mindless state, becoming an instance of 5245-2. Substance samples from 5245-2 seem to have levels of serotonin, oxytocin, and dopamine far higher than normal humans or SCP-5245. 5245-2 instances obey entirely the commands 5245 gives them, and are able themselves to create more instances of 5245-2 through a similar method, but lack the ability to speak. Destruction of these instances by shattering the mask beyond mundane repair causes SCP-5245 intense pain.</p>
<p>SCP-5245 has a humanoid intellect, speaking primarily German, but it is also fairly fluent in English. It uses abnormal speech patterns in both languages, however, using plural pronouns when referring to only itself. It answers well to its number, but also to the name “Mask” or the German “Maske,” and doesn’t seem to prefer any gendered pronouns over any other. 5245 is nearly constantly cheery, having levels of serotonin, oxytocin, and dopamine higher than normal humans, but angers fairly easily. It seems to be obsessed with happiness and joy, and many of our personnel describe its personality as charming and creative, but obsessive and blunt. Despite levels of intelligence on par with the average adult, with an IQ of 97, its interests are childish, and it finds games and media aimed towards late teen or adult audiences to be boring while enjoying children’s media and toys, with the exception being a preference for high-quality art materials. It also has a vast knowledge of art, both practical techniques and literal information, that it doesn’t claim to know the source of. It has been known to construct non-anomalous ceramic sculptures when bored, and enjoys making and painting them. Any ceramic forms produced may be separated from SCP-5245 at researcher discrescion.</p>
<p>SCP-5245 was found on November 25, 2019 in an abandoned house previously belonging to semi-influential Are We Cool Yet member Adelaide Schneider in Düsseldorf, Germany, shortly after a series of missing persons incidents. Inside a number of instances of 5245-2 were found, along with SCP-5245. In the same house were 4 journals, with sketches, descriptions of anomalous artistic concepts, and towards the end of the final journal, what seems to be concept drawings for 5245. These notebooks are disorganized, with scribbles and strikethroughs present throughout.</p>
<p>The final page, translated from German, reads:</p>
<div class="blockquote">
<p>To those who find us,<br />
A statement and a solution, my final piece is my magnum opus. I have put on a mask of happiness for so long my face doesn’t feel like mine. I’ve ruined my body so much it will never be pure again. My mind turned to black sludge, I let it flow until it puddled thick around me. So I made it official- we are now one, the sludge, the mask and I, and in this form, maybe we can finally be happy, finally be useful to the world, finally enjoy our existence, and spread joy. Offer a means of escape. A different black void than one of death.<br />
We are happy pills without the side effects. We are suicide without death. And maybe, just maybe, we are cool.<br />
Signed, Adelaide Schneider.</p>
</div>
<p><strong>Test Log:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>SCP-5245 was introduced to a D-Class with blue face paint applied. 5245 paused for a moment, seemingly confused, before turning him into an instance of 5245-2.</li>
<li>SCP-5245 was given a photo of a D-Class with a clear face. 5245 showed interest in it, saying it had a mild, “ignorable” compulsion to put an instance of 5245-1 on the D-Class and had never really seen a human face for very long, but otherwise was unaffected.</li>
<li>D-680315 was told he would be briefly allowed to see his family again if he survived this test, and was told that to survive, he must remain happy and display it outwardly- the promise of seeing his family was implemented so that he would display genuine happiness. He was then presented to SCP-5245 without his face covered. D-680315 was able to converse with an increasingly uncomfortable SCP-5245 for a minute and 37 seconds, at which point D-680315 dropped his smile for 1.76 seconds. Upon this, SCP-5245 attempted to and succeeded in turning him into an instance of 5245-2.</li>
<li>SCP-5245 was presented with a dog of mixed breed that had been abused prior to coming into foundation custody, and was suffering from PTSD as diagnosed by a qualified veterinarian. 5245 immediately took to the dog, attempting to pet and play with it. The dog was cautious around 5245, and attempted to attack it by biting into the substance. At this point, 5245 formed a mask (seemingly more of its own free will than previous times) and placed it on the dog, who transformed as was typical. The new 5245-2 instance was slightly more energetic than humanoid instances and showed none of the previous signs of trauma.</li>
<li>SCP-5245 was asked to create a ceramic sculpture of a human figure, to test its skill. 5245 asked for a model and was given a photo of Dr. Newman, the researcher in charge of its containment. An hour later, 5245 produced an abstract, 12&#160;cm tall sculpture of the doctor, technically well-produced and with more detail than was expected. 5245 reported dissatisfaction with the model, reporting that it “was better with animals” and “never really did a face before.” This sculpture shows no anomalous properties and has since been glazed for preservation purposes and placed on the doctor’s desk.</li>
</ul>
<p><em>Note: Are we sure this last one wasn’t just so she could have the statue? -Dr. ████</em></p>
<p><strong>Intake interview</strong><br />
<strong>Date:</strong> November 27, 2019</p>
<div class="blockquote">
<p>Dr. Newman: Hello, SCP-5245.</p>
<p>SCP-5245: Hi hi hi! When can we go back home? We left behind some sculptures we were working on.</p>
<p>Dr. Newman: I’m sorry, but you won’t be allowed.</p>
<p>SCP-5245: (The subject goes silent for a moment.) Oh. Can you at least get the models?</p>
<p>Dr. Newman: We’ll try to give them back when we’re done observing them, yes.</p>
<p>SCP-5245: What about our happy friends?</p>
<p>Dr. Newman: What do you mean?</p>
<p>SCP-5245: The ones with the masks.</p>
<p>Dr. Newman: We can’t tell you everything, I’m afraid, but they probably won’t be returned to you. How did they come to be?</p>
<p>SCP-5245: We made them!</p>
<p>Dr. Newman: We’ve observed the process in the field, yes, but Why did you make them?</p>
<p>SCP-5245: They… they were sad. They didn’t have the happy. So we gave them the happy! Happy happy happy friends, yes!</p>
<p>Dr. Newman: You took away their minds. Everything that made them human.</p>
<p>SCP-5245: And now they’re happy! Perfect happy friends!</p>
<p>Dr. Newman: I see. And that’s more important?</p>
<p>SCP-5245: Mm-hmm! That’s what everyone wants, right? Happy?</p>
<p>Dr. Newman: Some people want that. Others are interested in learning things or-</p>
<p>SCP-5245: (Interrupting) Because it makes them happy. Everyone should have happy! Happy, happy, happy, happy.</p>
<p>Dr. Newman: Ah. That makes some sense. Can you tell me, more specifically, why you feel so compelled that you won’t stop, even if it means hurting others?</p>
<p>SCP-5245: (The subject goes tense, briefly pausing.) We… we don’t know. It just happens. Can’t help ourselves. We guess it just feels too good to put a smile on someone’s face! Is the guard man okay by the way?</p>
<p>Dr. Newman: He’s got a bruise, but is otherwise unharmed. We’ve seen much worse.</p>
<p>SCP-5245: Good. Hurting people is sad.</p>
<p>Dr. Newman: It is.</p>
<p><em>(The rest of the interview has been omitted for brevity.)</em></p>
</div>
<p>Addendum:<br />
In an interview on May 16, 2020, SCP-5245 expressed a level of self-awareness that hadn’t previously been observed. Its senior researcher, Dr. Newman, had grown fond of it by the time the following transcript occurred in the middle of an interview:</p>
<div class="blockquote">
<p>SCP-5245: Thank you for talking with us, doctor! We… we’re really happy you came back.</p>
<p>Dr. Newman: I’m happy to talk with you, 5245. You’re very nice. Something seems to be bothering you today, is something wrong?</p>
<p>SCP-5245: A little. Just… please don’t stop talking when you’re done studying us.</p>
<p>Dr. Newman: I won’t, but why are you afraid we will?</p>
<p>SCP-5245: It’s just, we’ve never had real people to talk to. Our happy friends aren’t very good talkers. We guess we’re kinda lonely. But- But not anymore! You make us feel all kinds of happy! Happy happy happy happy!</p>
<p>Dr. Newman: Well thank you. You make me happy too.</p>
<p>SCP-5245: One more thing, miss… um… please don’t let us make you a happy friend. We know everyone should be happy and it’s really selfish not to let you be, but… you’re kinda like our only real friend.</p>
<p>Dr. Newman: I don’t mind. I like talking to you too, 5245.</p>
<p>SCP-5245: Okay. Thank you!</p>
</div>
<p>The conversation continued as normal from here.</p>
<p><strong>Addendum:</strong> Incident SCP-5245-A, June 5, 2020<br />
The former head researcher, now known as SCP-5245-2A, has been transferred to a separate containment. Dr. Newman, according to the anomaly, willingly allowed herself to be turned into an instance of 5245-2 after being struck with a series of intense personal tragedies. The psychologist that cleared her has been reprimanded. The research department is attempting to restore 5245-2A to her former state, or at least one of sentience, as she was a key factor in controlling SCP-5245’s behavior. If successful, 5245-2A will still be considered an SCP, and will likely be contained in junction with SCP-5245, as a way of containing 5245. 5245 has become manic and obsessive compulsive since her transformation, playing repetitively with statues of itself and the doctor in her previous state, with random periods of laughing, threatening staff, and rocking while repeating the German word for happy. The quality of its statues has also decreased, and it has disposed of several for not meeting its standards. A proposal to use amnestics to calm 5245 was denied, as it was determined that more could be learned about SCP-5245 by studying it in this state of grief.</p>
<p>The event was followed with an interview:</p>
<div class="blockquote">
<p>Dr. █████: SCP-5245. Explain yourself. Why did you do this to Dr. Newman?</p>
<p>SCP-5245: (No verbal communication.)</p>
<p>Dr. █████: SCP-5245, this is a major violation of-</p>
<p>SCP-5245: (interrupting) She made us.</p>
<p>Dr. █████: Explain, please.</p>
<p>SCP-5245: She had so much sad. We could feel it. We asked and she told us about her sad. And then she turned off the red light, and asked if we could stop her sad.</p>
<p>(This is corroborated by the audio recording, which stops abruptly after the doctor explains her emotional pain to SCP-5245.)</p>
<p>Dr. █████: (morosely) I see. So you masked her?</p>
<p>SCP-5245: uh huh. She said she’d hurt herself if we didn’t. Hurting people is sad. She said being a person didn’t matter if she wasn’t happy. We kinda agree, but… she… she promised. She promised to be our friend. But we couldn’t stop ourselves, we… we guess it was selfish to even try to argue. She deserved our happy.</p>
<p>Dr. █████: Thank you for clarifying. We’ll assign a new head researcher as quick as we can.</p>
<p><em>SCP-5245 sniffles, a thin black liquid identical to the substance begins to leak from the mask’s eyes.</em></p>
<p>SCP-5245: We don’t wanna do this. It hurts, it hurts a lot. We just wanna be cool. We just wanna help people. We were made to be happy, but we… we don’t have any friends. We just want one friend forever. But we… don’t deserve it.</p>
<p>Dr. █████: (now uncomfortable) I’m sorry, 5245. Talk to your containment psychiatrist. Maybe they can get a dog or something for you. And if it helps, we noticed that neurotransmitters associated with happiness were highly elevated in her composition.</p>
<p>SCP-5245: Right. Happy. It’s… its okay. It’s okay! (It suddenly becomes cheerful.) she’s happy, isn’t she? Happy happy happy happy! And nothing will ever hurt her again! That’s what’s important! (Black liquid continues to leak from the mask’s eyes as it breaks into laughter.) it’d be selfish to keep it from her! It’s our purpose, and we’d do it too if we could! Happy happy happy forever!</p>
<p>Dr. █████: 5245, are you okay?</p>
<p>SCP-5245: uh-huh! It’s all okay, dandy fine doo, yes yes yes! Because making her happy makes us happy! That’s why we exist, right?</p>
<p>Dr. █████: please stop. You’re starting to scare me.</p>
<p>(SCP-5245 laughs and begins to repeat the German word for happy in a sing-song manner for the remaining duration of the tape, during which Dr. █████ leaves abruptly.)</p>
</div>
